  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAreas of fog will be possible this morning given the moisture added \nfrom recent showers, calm winds, and clearing skies. The valleys \nhave the greatest potential for dense fog as visibilities are \nexpected to drop below a mile. Fog will linger through daybreak, \nslowly clearing out after sunrise.\n\nIsolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms will be possible \nonce again today. These diurnally driven showers and storms will \nhave additional support from weak waves passing along a broad \ntrough. The MLCAPE will be around 500 to 1000 J/kg, but 0-6 km bulk \nshear will be 20 kts or less suggesting a pulse storm convective \nmode. As seen in model soundings, there is little wind to mix down \nlater today. Instability does extend into the hail growth zone so \nsmall hail would be possible. Given the weak shear though, severe \nstorms are not expected. With weak flow aloft, storms may be slow \nmoving. Despite PWATs only peaking at 1.5 inches today, localized \nflash flooding may be possible if a slow moving storm develops over \nan urban or poor drainage area, similarly to what happened in the \neastern Syracuse suburbs last evening. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAs the upper level trough digs over the Northeast Monday night into \nTuesday, a stacked low pressure system begins to take shape. This \nsystem will bring widespread showers and isolated thunderstorms to \nthe region Tuesday morning. Models show instability increasing by \nthe afternoon hours, mainly east of I-81. That may be possible if \nshowers a slow to spread eastward and there is some morning \nsunshine. However, model guidance have trended slightly faster, so \nthe instability looks to be overdone. Even with good instability, \nshear is looking weak once again with the exception of portions of \nNortheast PA where 0-6km shear would be around 30 kts. \n\nThis system will spin over the region and slightly enhanced LLJ out \nof the south will advect in moisture from the Atlantic Tuesday \nafternoon and into Tuesday night. This is when PWAT values will peak \nwith values between 1.75 and 2 inches. In addition, the Corfidi \nUpshear vector speed is low, suggesting that training of showers and \nstorms may be possible during this time as well. This and orographic \nenhancement could enhance rainfall amounts over a short period of \ntime and lead to localized flash flooding. Rivers and streams have \nbeen low for awhile now and have taken on any recent rainfall well. \nEnsemble forecasts have non-zero chances for our typical trouble \nspots to reach action. There does remain uncertainty where the \nhighest rainfall amounts will be though and these forecasts may \nchange. WPC has a slight risk for excessive rainfall for parts of \nthe region on Tuesday, which seems reasonable given the overall set \nup. \n\nA dry slot will try to nudge its way into the region Wednesday \nmorning and bring a break from the showers for some. However, this \nlow slowly drifts eastward and will keep wraparound moisture over \nthe region through at least the daytime hours on Thursday before the \nsystem slides off and drier air moves in. PWATs will not be as high \nas Tuesday during this time but additional rain on already saturated \nareas may further enhance any issues the develop. WPC maintains a \nMarginal Risk through Wednesday.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...\n\nWith surface high pressure building into the region, it will be a \npleasant end to the work week. Unfortunately, this dry period may be \nbrief as another upper level trough approaches the region Saturday. \nThere remains uncertainty on the shape of this trough and how much \nit may dig over the region. Shower coverage may be scattered on \nSaturday before becoming more widespread on Sunday as the trough \nmoves through.",
